        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Chandragupta Maurya</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 320 BCE – c. 298 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">Year and place unknown; there are no historical facts about his origins and early life, only legends. The year given here, c. 320 BCE, is the conventional start of his reign. Greek and Roman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Buddhist and Jain sources give differing accounts of his family</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">Date, place and circumstances unclear and disputed; according to Roy, he abdicated c. 298 BCE and died between 297 and 293 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> Jain tradition says he fasted to death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, a story that Sushma Jansari calls late and problematic</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Founder and first emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>; reigned c. 320 BCE – c. 298 BCE (scholars give dates from 324 or 322 to 297 or 293 BCE)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">overthrowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda dynasty</a> and founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>; the war against Alexander's Greek governors in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>; the marriage alliance and exchange of 500 elephants with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>; a decentralised administration with provinces and a council of advisers; irrigation works and roads; his partnership with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a> in the legends; the Jain story of his renunciation</span></div>
        </div>
