    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Chandragupta rose in the unrest that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shahs-invasion-of-india">Indian campaign</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, though the order and dates of these events remain debated.</li><li>He made a dynastic marriage alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> c. 305–303 BCE, receiving the regions along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> and sending 500 elephants in exchange, and ruled through a decentralised administration of provinces and local governments.</li><li>His life reaches us only through Greek and Roman fragments and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Buddhist and Jain texts written centuries later, which disagree on his origins, his rise and his death, so historians treat most of the narrative with caution.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Sir William Jones proposed in 1793 that the Indian king &quot;Sandracottus&quot; of the Greek and Roman writers was Chandragupta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> texts, which made it possible to match Indian and Graeco-Roman history; his reign has been called &quot;the sheet anchor of Indian chronology&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

