    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 326 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> ends his Indian campaign in 326 to 325 BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> later claims that Chandragupta, as a young man, saw him</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">323 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander dies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and war breaks out between his generals, leaving unrest in the Indian territories he had assigned to Greek governors</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 322 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Chandragupta's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> and the defeat of the Nandas is generally dated between 322 and 319 BCE, though a later chronology places it c. 311–305 BCE</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 317 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Jansari dates Chandragupta's arrival in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> here, in line with the chronology of Greek history</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 305 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Chandragupta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> confront each other and then, by about 303 BCE, form a dynastic marriage alliance</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 298 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">According to Roy, Chandragupta's abdication may be dated here</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 297 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">According to Roy, Chandragupta dies at some point between 297 and 293 BCE</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>There are no historical facts about Chandragupta's origins and early life, only legends, and the narrative of his reign is deduced mainly from a few fragments in Greek and Roman sources and a few Indian religious texts, all written centuries after his death. None of the ancient texts says when he was born. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> claimed that he saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> as a young man during Alexander's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, c. 326–325 BCE, and on that assumption Raychaudhuri proposed in 1923 that he may have been born after 350 BCE.</p><p>The sources differ on his family. The Roman historian Justin, in the 2nd century CE, says he was of humble origin. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> texts make him a son of one of the Nanda king's wives, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mur-river">Mura</a>, or of a concubine; the 11th-century Kashmiri texts, the Kathasaritsagara and the Brihat-Katha-Manjari, make him a son of Purva-Nanda. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> play Mudrarakshasa calls him Vrishala, a word that can mean the son of a shudra or the best of kings; Radha Kumud Mukherjee argued for the second sense. Buddhist texts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>, written about seven centuries after his dynasty ended, describe him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> of the Moriya clan, a branch of the Buddha's Shakya family, and explain the name Maurya by peacocks (mora in Pali). These sources seek to link the dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> to the Buddha.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Chandragupta Maurya&#8221; cites where it covers Historical background, Early life:</p><ul><li>Radha Kumud Mookerji, Chandragupta Maurya and his times (Motilal Banarsidass, 1988)</li><li>Hemacandra, The Lives of the Jain Elders (Oxford University Press, 1998)</li><li>Kallidaikurichi Aiyah Nilakanta Sastri, Age of the Nandas and Mauryas (Motilal Banarsidass, 1988)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/bindusara">Bindusara<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The political setting was the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda dynasty</a> around 350 BCE, followed by Alexander's campaign in the north-west, which he ended in 325 BCE after a mutiny. Alexander assigned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> territories to Greek governors and died in 323 BCE. According to Greek and Roman texts, Chandragupta then attacked the governors during a period of unrest and local warfare and took control of the eastern Indus Valley. Justin writes that Alexander's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">prefects</a> had been assassinated and that Chandragupta, the author of this liberation, himself oppressed the people he had freed after taking the throne. Justin also says he offended the Nanda king, who ordered his execution, and that he saved himself by flight; he adds omens, a lion that licked the sleeping Chandragupta and a wild elephant that offered itself as his steed.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> play Mudrarakshasa, composed probably between 300 and 700 CE, and Buddhist and Jain legends bring in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> counsellor who in some versions was insulted by the Nanda king and swore to destroy him. In the Jain account by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemachandra">Hemachandra</a>, written nearly 1,400 years after Chandragupta's death, the army was raised with coins Chanakya minted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Jain texts say the war with the Nandas was bitterly fought; Buddhist accounts describe an easy victory. The Nanda king was defeated and exiled in some accounts and killed in the Buddhist ones. Historically reliable details of the campaign are unavailable.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Chandragupta Maurya&#8221; cites where it covers Rise to power:</p><ul><li>Radha Kumud Mookerji, Chandragupta Maurya and his times (Motilal Banarsidass, 1988)</li><li>Hemacandra, The Lives of the Jain Elders (Oxford University Press, 1998)</li><li>Kaushik Roy, Hinduism and the Ethics of Warfare in South Asia: From Antiquity to the Present (Cambridge University Press, 2012)</li><li>Irfan Habib, Mauryan India (Aligarh Historians Society / Tulika Books, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator.jpg" alt="Seleucus I Nicator" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seleukos_I_Nikator_Bronze_Roman_100BCE-100CE_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_Naples_AN_5590_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allan Gluck, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> is generally dated to c. 322–319 BCE and his expansion into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> to c. 317–312 BCE. Some scholars have suggested that he first built his power in the Punjab, and an alternative chronology based on Justin puts it all in c. 311–305 BCE. Between 305 and 303 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>, who had founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> in 312 BCE, tried to retake the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. The two formed a dynastic marriage alliance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, writing about 300 years later, says Seleucus gave the Indus lands to Sandrocottus on terms of intermarriage and in exchange for 500 elephants. The extent of the land is unknown; a modest reading limits it to the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>, the Punjab and eastern Gandhara, and scholars such as Coningham and Young question control of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and the lower Indus. Seleucus sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> as ambassador, who stayed four years. Rudradaman's inscription at Junagadh, about 150 CE, shows Chandragupta's rule reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, where his governor Vaishya Pushyagupta had the Sudarshana lake built.</p><p>The administration was decentralised, with provinces, local governments and a council of advisers. According to Megasthenes, three bodies handled villages, cities and the military. Chandragupta is credited with irrigation works and roads, including a highway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> described by Megasthenes. Most scholars now think the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a> is not of Mauryan origin.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Chandragupta Maurya&#8221; cites where it covers Administration:</p><ul><li>Radha Kumud Mookerji, Chandragupta Maurya and his times (Motilal Banarsidass, 1988)</li><li>Kallidaikurichi Aiyah Nilakanta Sastri, Age of the Nandas and Mauryas (Motilal Banarsidass, 1988)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/porus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porus.jpg" alt="Porus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porus_alexander_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">User PHGCOM, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Accounts of Chandragupta come mostly from sources with their own aims. The Brahmanical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, from Gupta times, viewed the Nandas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a> as illegitimate rulers because of their shudra background. Justin, a Roman writer of the 2nd century CE, accuses Chandragupta of turning the liberation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> peoples into servitude. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> calls him a great king, but not as great in power and influence as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> or Agrammes, the Greek name for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhana-nanda">Dhana Nanda</a>.</p><p>The Jain accounts hold the conquests responsible for suffering. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> legend the monk Bhadrabahu foretold a twelve-year famine because of the killing and violence of Chandragupta's conquests, and led monks south. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemachandra">Hemachandra</a>'s Svetambara legend Chandragupta followed non-Jain ascetics with "the wrong view of religion" and lusted for women for fifteen years before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a> persuaded him to turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>; the legend also has Chanakya use violence and cunning to expand the empire and raise revenue. Contemporary Greek evidence says he drank wine, performed Vedic sacrifices and animal sacrifices and enjoyed hunting, which does not suit Jain non-violence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a>'s prescriptions are incompatible with his reign and that Chanakya's identification with Kautilya dates from a later period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahavira"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahavira.jpg" alt="Mahavira" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahavir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dayodaya, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No ancient text gives the end of his reign. According to some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist texts he ruled 24 years. Buddhist sources place his reign 162 years after the Buddha's death, a chronology that differs greatly from the Greek and Roman record, and Jain sources give differing gaps after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>'s death. Historians give different dates for the reign: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irfan-habib">Irfan Habib</a> and Vivekanand Jha c. 322–298 BCE, Upinder Singh 324 or 321 to 297 BCE and Kristi Wiley 320 to 293 BCE. According to Roy, he abdicated c. 298 BCE and died between 297 and 293 BCE.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> Jain tradition says he gave up his empire to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bindusara">Bindusara</a> and joined Bhadrabahu as a monk. They went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, where he lived as an ascetic for several years and then fasted to death in the ritual of sallekhana. The tradition is recorded in texts from the 10th century onward, and Jain inscriptions from the 7th to 15th centuries name Bhadrabahu and a Prabhacandra whom later tradition identified as Chandragupta. The doubts are several: the Svetambara texts place Bhadrabahu near the Himalayan foothills, one Digambara version has Samprati Chandragupta, a later Maurya, perform the ritual, and Sushma Jansari concludes that the story developed after about 600 CE.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Chandragupta Maurya&#8221; cites where it covers Jain accounts of renunciation and retirement in Karnataka:</p><ul><li>Radha Kumud Mookerji, Chandragupta Maurya and his times (Motilal Banarsidass, 1988)</li><li>V. R. Ramachandra Dikshitar, The Mauryan Polity (Motilal Banarsidass Publ., 1993)</li><li>Kristi L. Wiley, The A to Z of Jainism (Scarecrow, 2009)</li><li>Hemacandra, The Lives of the Jain Elders (Oxford University Press, 1998)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chandragupta's empire, which reached its peak under his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, began an era of economic prosperity, reforms and infrastructure expansion. Under his dynasty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajivika">Ajivika</a> gained prominence beside other traditions. After his reign, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a>'s ambassador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> left an account of his court, and Greek and Roman authors who quoted it are among the main sources on him. Without them, according to Mookerji, this period would be "a most obscure chapter of Indian history".</p><p>The identification proposed by William Jones in 1793 made the dating of early Indian history possible, and his reign has been called "the sheet anchor of Indian chronology". It is uncertain whether his rule reached southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, since the empire stretched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> by the time Ashoka took the throne in c. 268 BCE and the conquests may be his or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bindusara">Bindusara</a>'s. Sushma Jansari notes that views diverged in the 20th century: western academics tend to take a reserved view of his accomplishments, while Indian authors have portrayed him as a very successful king who established the first Indian nation. A memorial to him stands on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragiri">Chandragiri</a> hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a>, and he has been the subject of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Bengali plays and later films and television series.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Chandragupta Maurya (reigned c. 320 – c. 298 BCE) founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> after defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda dynasty</a> and taking the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>, and formed an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>. His life is known only from late and partisan Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Buddhist and Jain sources, which disagree on his origins and death.</em></p>

